If by “that family” you mean systems that expose or manipulate latent sensory channels, then the things that prey on them are basically sensory exploiters: organisms, environments, and technologies that learn the decoder well enough to feed it misleading inputs.

A few major classes:

* Sensory traps — exploit a response that evolved for something else. Anglerfish lures, flowers mimicking mates, brood parasites exploiting parental cues.
* Supernormal stimuli — exaggerate a valid cue beyond anything normally encountered. Hyper-saturated colors, oversized signals, extreme sweetness, notification sounds, pornography, junk food.
* Mimicry — reproduce just enough of the pattern to trigger recognition while hiding the true source. Batesian mimicry is the biological version; phishing and counterfeit UI are cognitive versions.
* Camouflage / countershading — rather than generating the desired percept, they prevent useful decomposition. They destroy edges, contours, motion cues, or figure-ground separation so the sensory system cannot recover the object.
* Signal jamming — overwhelm the channel. Loud noise masks speech; odor saturation masks weaker smells; visual clutter hides motion; electronic warfare does the same abstractly.
* Cross-modal spoofing — make one sense veto another. Visual mouth movement alters speech perception; packaging color changes flavor expectations; artificial motion cues can alter balance.
* Predictive-model attacks — exploit what the brain expects. Once the system strongly predicts an object, sparse evidence may be enough to make it “see” the expected thing.
* Habituation predators — repeat a stimulus until the detector stops caring, then exploit the lowered vigilance. Nature does this; so do alarms, advertising, spam, and security systems.
* Attention hijackers — they don’t defeat sensation; they monopolize the allocation mechanism upstream. Sudden movement, infant-like faces, screaming, bright contrast, novelty, social threat.
* Ambiguity parasites — live specifically in regions where multiple interpretations fit the same sensory evidence. Camouflage, optical illusions, ventriloquism, deceptive signaling, and many social manipulations belong here.

The deeper pattern is:

\text{receiver has decoder }D

An honest signal gives:

x \rightarrow D(x) \rightarrow \text{useful inference}

An exploiter learns enough about D to manufacture:

x^\* \rightarrow D(x^\*)=\text{desired false inference}

without having to reproduce the underlying reality.

That is probably the natural predator of the Sensory Prism concept: not something that attacks the prism itself, but something that learns which separated channels carry the most decision weight and then spoofs, masks, saturates, or reroutes those channels.

And there’s an interesting arms race:

\text{signal}
\rightarrow
\text{decoder}
\rightarrow
\text{exploiter}
\rightarrow
\text{better decoder}
\rightarrow
\text{better exploiter}

That shows up in vision, hearing, mating signals, immune systems, cybersecurity, propaganda, advertising, and even adversarial attacks on neural networks.

The Pantheon operator here might be Decoder Predation: anything that gains advantage by modeling another system’s perception rather than its underlying reality.